---
/**
 * P3 Governments: /partner-with-us/governments/ (T3, implements doc 04 matrix 4.2).
 * C1 to C14 in order, as the numbered table in doc 06 P3, without C3 (Cost of Waiting) and C4 (Imagine), which the audience pages leave to Home since WP47.
 * WP30c (owner direction 2026-10-14, "less is more"): the page keeps every CMF band and states each point once. Gone: the In Short summary and its jump links,
 * the fit check, the "Outside Bangladesh?", national-target, scale and coastal-context callouts, the survey callout, the second Her Power card set, the three-step
 * restatement of the mechanism, the standard-inclusions list and the four-point restatement of the scorecard. The H1 is the owner's wording.
 * WP35 (cycle-12 review): the trial and the survey are shown once, as tiles in Results at a Glance; the proof band that restated them is a one-line strip of links (#proof-band). The WSDFM tile is gone
 * from the glance (its 71% is program records, read beside the trial's 63.1%), and the plain-words fixes are in the contracts table and the services line.
 * WP36 (cycle-13 reviews): the 31,200+ is stated once, in Results at a Glance (the Track Record band's tile is gone; the contracts table below keeps each contract's own scope); the glance tile reads "People in the Scopes
 * of Six Government Contracts"; "What Your Agency Gets" speaks of spending "measured against outcomes" and a budget "assessed against the agreed scorecard"; the three pains are statements; the cost question has the
 * one-line route answer and a link to the hub FAQ.
 * WP37 (cycle-14 review): Her Power is stated once in full, in Results at a Glance (the tile links its case study) and in the contracts table's first row. The "Her Power, with the ICT Division" band is gone, replaced by a
 * "Read the Her Power Case Study" text link under the table, and the national-scale answer no longer retells Her Power: "We scale in phases. A 100,000+ national program is a design option we have not yet delivered.",
 * with its closing sentence linking the contracts table.
 * WP39 (cycle-16 review): "What Your Agency Gets" is three short cards (IncludesBand), not the concern / inclusion / benefit matrix; the contracts table's last column reads "Training Figure" (it mixes delivered and scoped figures).
 * WP41 (cycle-18 review): the contracts table's scope cells are short phrases in every row (Her Power's was a sentence with three figures), and every figure sits in the "Training Figure" column; the "Capacity That Stays" card of
 * the delivery band no longer repeats the manuals figure (the table keeps it).
 * WP42 (cycle-19 review): "Our Approach" is the two shared sentences with the reader named in the second ("Your agency receives monthly reports on progress toward paid work, as agreed in the program scope."; POSITIONING.governments, declared whole in scripts/check-facts.mjs).
 * WP46 (cycle-22 review): "What a Ministry Sees" is gone (it restated the reporting cadence the "Our Approach" statement, the delivery model and the FAQ already give); its one detail that stood nowhere else, the follow-up surveys at 3, 6 and 12 months
 * after each course (PD-09, read as TRACER_MONTHS), now sits in the delivery model's "Placement and Monthly Reports" card.
 * WP47 (owner decision 2026-10-15): the page opens with its own proof and goes straight to its offer. The Cost of Waiting band ("Without Tracking, Agencies Cannot See What Training Delivers") and the Imagine band
 * ("What Your Next Budget Cycle Could Show") are gone; Home carries them. Nothing on this page depended on them: the reporting and tracking claims stay in "Our Approach", the delivery model and
 * "Spending Measured Against Outcomes"; the capacity claim stays in "Capacity That Stays" and "Capacity Designed to Stay in Your System". The "Graduates Struggle to Find Work" card says "A degree alone does not guarantee a job offer."
 * WP48 (cycle-23 review): the contracts table's last column reads "Scale and Outputs" (it was "Training Figure"; the cells hold people, scopes and distributed documents). The cells are unchanged. The glance is headed "Results and
 * Contract Scope at a Glance" (it was "Results at a Glance"; its first tile is the contract scope).
 * WP49 (cycle-24 reviews): two of the "What Gets in the Way" lines say what the agency needs ("Track who is earning afterward, not only who enrolled.", "National delivery needs trained facilitators and localized curricula.") in place of
 * sector-wide generalizations; the page's action is a ministry briefing, so its cost answer (Q8-government) and the note in its "Choose Where to Start" lead say "in the briefing" (PROGRAM_SIZES_NOTE_BRIEFING), not "in the discovery session";
 * that lead no longer repeats the cost answer's pricing sentence (the "What Does It Cost?" answer below states it); "What Your Agency Gets" speaks of completers, not graduates (the site's word for people who finished a program);
 * the fourth glance tile (Kosovo) is part of "Results and Contract Scope at a Glance" (a single tile needs no "More Results" heading); the steps band is "What Happens Next".
 * WP50 (cycle-25 reviews): Her Power's three lines in the contracts table are full sentences (ContractTable); the closing "Government partners include ..." line is gone, because the Track Record band's logo strip names the same five bodies;
 * the "How Fast Can We Start?" answer (Q7-government) is the procurement sentence alone ("Public procurement follows the agency’s own timeline, so the first step is a briefing for your ministry."), without the grant-pilot timing;
 * the call-to-action band's text link reads "See YouthWIDE for Governments" (it read "See government programs"; "Explore Our Programs" mislabelled this YouthWIDE destination); its destination, the Bangladesh band of the YouthWIDE page (LINKS.nationwide), is unchanged.
 * WP51 (cycle-26 reviews): the first glance tile's note is "The figure is contract scope, not a count of completers. The contracts table below shows each scope." (a page-level variant of GV-12's note: Her Power's two figures are in that table; Impact keeps the full
 * note), so its data-fact lists GV-12 and SC-06 only; the Track Record band's empathy line (EMPATHY.governments) says the budgets "must produce young people in work, not only seats filled".
 * WP52 (cycle-27 review): the Her Power glance tile's label names the program ("Her Power: tracked women in work (47%), among the 2,500 women CodersTrust trained directly"; the tile showed "372 of 800" without it); the delivery model's
 * "Placement and Monthly Reports" card scopes its reports and surveys ("..., as agreed in the program scope.").
 * WP52 (cycle-27 reviews, Claude): the delivery model's third card is "Capacity Designed to Stay" (it was "Capacity That Stays", a promise with no scope; the card below it already says "Capacity Designed to Stay in Your System"); the closing call to action no longer
 * repeats the procurement point (its urgency line and the first-action line that closed the page are gone; the "How Fast Can We Start?" answer and the steps band state it); the contracts-table note excepts the Her Power figures from "not delivered scale".
 * WP52 (cycle-27 reviews, Grok M2 and Gemini m6): the Track Record band no longer opens with the empathy line ("You answer for training budgets that must produce young people in work, not only seats filled."); it starts with the origin line.
 * WP52 (cycle-27 review, Gemini P1): the "Choose Where to Start" band's eyebrow is "Options" (it was the shared default, "Start", which echoed the heading's last word); Development Partners and Universities, which paired the same eyebrow with the same heading, changed with it.
 * WP54 (cycle-28 reviews, GPT-6.1 Sol G2 and Claude S20): the third pain is "Trainer and curriculum capacity takes time to build" (it said capacity "is thin", which presumed a deficiency; the line under it is unchanged); the NHRDF contract's name says
 * "implemented by" in lower case (it was "Implemented by", a capital in the middle of a parenthesis; see ContractTable).
 * WP60 (cycle-32 review, Claude S6): the delivery model's third card is "Trainers and Curriculum" (it was "Capacity Designed to Stay", one letter-run from the benefit card "Capacity Designed to Stay in Your System" in "What Your Agency Gets"); its text is unchanged,
 * and the capacity claim stays in the benefit card.
 * WP60 (cycle-32 review, Gemini GM5 and GM8): the benefit card "Capacity Designed to Stay in Your System" opens "Where included in your program scope:" (the list that follows read as unconditional; the rest of the list is unchanged); the learner card shows one line,
 * "Won logo-design work on Freelancer.com, then gained more clients." (its narrative sentence repeated it, and is not a quotation, so it is gone; LearnerVoice takes no excerpt here).
 * WP61 (cycle-33 review, GLM L5): "Results and Contract Scope at a Glance" leads with the results and ends with the contract scope: the randomized trial (+10.3), Her Power (372 of 800), Kosovo, then the 30,000+ contract scope (it led with the contract scope). The tiles, labels and notes are unchanged;
 * the contract-scope tile is the single secondary tile, which PartnerGlance draws as the fourth main tile (WP49).
 * WP63 (cycle-35 reviews, Gemini M6 and GLM L6): the Her Power glance tile's label states the tracked base ("Her Power: 47% of the 800 tracked women in work, from the 2,500 women CodersTrust trained directly"; HER_POWER_TILE_LABEL);
 * the contracts table is headed "What Each Government Contract Covers" (it was "Six Government Contracts, Scope by Scope"). The table's anchor (#contracts) is unchanged.
 * WP66 (owner request 2026-10-20): the Track Record band's list closes with the signed-agreement sentence for government bodies (SC-11a, `signed` on PartnerGuideBand: "CodersTrust has signed MOUs, agreements or contracts with ... government bodies."); the band's heading says "Since 2014", so the sentence leaves the year to it.
 */
import PartnerGlance from '../../../components/pages/partners/PartnerGlance.astro';
import PairedEvidence from '../../../components/pages/partners/PairedEvidence.astro';
import DueDiligenceView from '../../../components/pages/hub/DueDiligenceView.astro';
import LearnerVoice from '../../../components/pages/impact/LearnerVoice.astro';
import LandingLayout from '../../../layouts/LandingLayout.astro';
import HeroBand from '../../../components/sections/HeroBand.astro';
import DefinitionBand from '../../../components/sections/DefinitionBand.astro';
import ProblemBand from '../../../components/sections/ProblemBand.astro';
import MechanismBand from '../../../components/sections/MechanismBand.astro';
import IncludesBand from '../../../components/sections/IncludesBand.astro';
import FAQBand from '../../../components/sections/FAQBand.astro';
import RiskReversalBand from '../../../components/sections/RiskReversalBand.astro';
import CTABand from '../../../components/ui/CTABand.astro';
import SessionAgenda from '../../../components/ui/SessionAgenda.astro';
import GainsPains from '../../../components/pages/partners/GainsPains.astro';
import PartnerGuideBand from '../../../components/pages/partners/PartnerGuideBand.astro';
import SubBand from '../../../components/pages/partners/SubBand.astro';
import EvidenceList from '../../../components/pages/partners/EvidenceList.astro';
import ContractTable from '../../../components/pages/partners/ContractTable.astro';
import RouteLink from '../../../components/pages/partners/RouteLink.astro';
import OfferCards from '../../../components/pages/partners/OfferCards.astro';
import StepsBand from '../../../components/pages/partners/StepsBand.astro';
import PageEnhancements from '../../../components/pages/partners/PageEnhancements.astro';
import { KOSOVO_WOW_TILE_LABEL, LINKS, TRACER_MONTHS, funderOptionCards, meta } from '../../../components/pages/partners/partners';
import { CONTRACT_TOTAL_NOTE, HEADLINE, POSITIONING, PROGRAM_SIZES_NOTE_BRIEFING, VALUE_PROPOSITION, WB_KOSOVO } from '../../../data/copy';
import { HER_POWER_TILE_LABEL } from '../../../components/pages/home/caseStudies';
import { faqJsonLd } from '../../../lib/faq';
import { cta } from '../../../lib/mailto';
import { webPage } from '../../../lib/schema';

const PATH = '/partner-with-us/governments/';
const primary = cta('briefing');

const { title, description } = meta(
  'Digital Skills Training for Governments',
  // Doc 06 P3 meta (R10/WP11 F17): the value proposition, with no count. SC-06's 31,200+ needs its footnote, which a meta description cannot carry.
  VALUE_PROPOSITION.governments,
);

// The Her Power tile's label is a page-level label (HER_POWER_TILE_LABEL, built from PR-05 in caseStudies.ts; the shared card caption and the register's PR-05 are unchanged). The cards on the Development Partners and Foundations pages sit under the program's name; this tile does not,
// so the label opens with it (WP52, cycle-27 review: "372 of 800" alone did not name the program). WP63 (cycle-35 review, Gemini M6): and it states the tracked base itself, "47% of the 800 tracked women in work, from the 2,500 women CodersTrust trained directly"
// (it said "tracked women in work (47%), among the 2,500 women ...", which could be read as all 2,500 tracked); the count, 372, is the tile's own figure above the label.
// WP51 (cycle-26 review, Kimi m2): Her Power's 25,125 and 2,500 are in the contracts table below, so this page's tile did not repeat them (its note was the register note's first sentence and a pointer to the table; Impact keeps the full note).
// WP57 (cycle-29 review, Grok R2, with Gemini GM1): the tile's note is the shared CONTRACT_TOTAL_NOTE ("The total is contract scope, not a count of completers; it includes Her Power’s national program (25,125 women trained)."), so the total visibly
// includes Her Power's national program; the pointer sentence is gone (the tile links to the table), and the tile's data-fact lists GV-12, SC-06 and GV-01. The 25,125 is GV-01's value, read in src/data/copy.ts.
// WP58 (cycle-30 review, Gemini GM1): the note's parenthesis says who trained whom, "(25,125 women trained; CodersTrust trained 2,500 directly)", so the tile's data-fact also lists GV-11 (CONTRACT_TOTAL_NOTE.facts, which holds the 2,500).
const contractsNote = CONTRACT_TOTAL_NOTE.text;
const contractsNoteFacts = CONTRACT_TOTAL_NOTE.facts.filter((f) => f !== 'GV-12');
// Q8-government: the one-line cost answer with a link to the partner hub FAQ, which keeps the full answer (WP36); it says "in the briefing", the first step on this page (WP49, cycle-24 review; the other route pages use Q8-route).
const FAQ_IDS = ['Q7-government', 'Q2-government', 'Q1-government', 'Q10', 'Q8-government'];
---

<LandingLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={PATH}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Partner with Us', href: LINKS.hub }, { label: 'Governments' }]}
  jsonLd={[webPage({ path: PATH, name: title, description }), faqJsonLd('governments', PATH, FAQ_IDS)]}
  stickyCta={{ label: primary.label, href: primary.href }}
>
  <!-- C1 · Identity hook -->
  <HeroBand
    audience="For Ministries and Public Agencies"
    title={HEADLINE.governments}
    lead={VALUE_PROPOSITION.governments}
    cta={{ label: primary.label, href: primary.href }}
    artSeed="partners-governments"
  />
  <RouteLink kicker="See the Program" label="How YouthWIDE Works" href={LINKS.youthwide} />
  <!-- GEO · What is CodersTrust? -->
  <DefinitionBand />
  <PartnerGlance
    title="Results and Contract Scope at a Glance"
    lead={[
      { id: 'RC-02', href: '/impact/independent-evaluation/' },
      { id: 'PR-05', href: '/impact/case-studies/her-power/', label: HER_POWER_TILE_LABEL },
      { id: 'PR-02', href: '/impact/case-studies/kosovo-women-in-online-work/', label: KOSOVO_WOW_TILE_LABEL, note: WB_KOSOVO.text, also: WB_KOSOVO.facts },
    ]}
    more={[{ id: 'GV-12', href: '/partner-with-us/governments/#contracts', label: 'People in the Scopes of Six Government Contracts', note: contractsNote, also: ['SC-06', ...contractsNoteFacts] }]}
  />
  <LearnerVoice story="maria-dewan" outcome="Won logo-design work on Freelancer.com, then gained more clients." program="Her Power" tone="white" />

  <!-- C2 · Problem. WP31: the heading speaks to any ministry. WP34 (cycle-11 review): so does the tile, the global PX-01 figure as on Home; the Bangladesh 13.5% (PX-03) stays on the Bangladesh pages. -->
  <ProblemBand
    title="Training Budgets Need Employment Results"
    bluf="Budgets that count enrollment cannot show who is earning."
    factIds={['PX-01']}
  >
    <GainsPains
      slot="lead"
      pains={[
        { q: 'Graduates struggle to find work', text: 'A degree alone does not guarantee a job offer.' },
        // WP49 (cycle-24 review): this line and the next say what the agency needs, not what is "rarely" so across a sector.
        { q: 'Training budgets do not show up as earnings', text: 'Track who is earning afterward, not only who enrolled.' },
        // WP38 (cycle-15 review): a general funder pain, stated in one line like its two neighbors; it rests on no CodersTrust fact.
        // WP54 (cycle-28 review, GPT-6.1 Sol G2): "takes time to build", not "is thin" (which said the sector's capacity is deficient); the band is still a barrier band, and the line under it is unchanged.
        { q: 'Trainer and curriculum capacity takes time to build', text: 'National delivery needs trained facilitators and localized curricula.' },
      ]}
    />
  </ProblemBand>

  <!-- C3 (Cost of Waiting) and C4 (Imagine) are left to Home since WP47 (owner decision 2026-10-15). -->

  <!-- C5 · Guide. WP52 (cycle-27 reviews, Grok M2 and Gemini m6): no empathy line; the band opens with the origin line ("Since 2014, ..."), since the problem band above states the problem (as Local Partners, Foundations and Development Partners). -->
  <PartnerGuideBand
    statements={[{ facts: ['SC-04'], markers: [], text: 'CodersTrust has been National University’s official training partner for digital career skills and credentials since 2023.' }]}
    signed="SC-11a"
    method={{ text: 'Our TalentLEAP method organizes skills into career paths.', link: { label: 'How TalentLEAP Works', href: LINKS.talentleap } }}
    partnerGroup={['government', 'academic']}
    strip
  />

  <!-- C6 · Mechanism (YouthWIDE-style delivery). WP76 (owner direction, the settled target-group wording): the "Who We Reach" card reads "Educated, Unemployed and Underemployed Youth", as on Home, YouthWIDE and the shared mechanism band; its body is unchanged. -->
  <MechanismBand
    title="A National Delivery Model, Built on Labs That Already Exist"
    positioning={POSITIONING.governments}
    blocks={[
      { label: 'Who We Reach', title: 'Educated, Unemployed and Underemployed Youth', text: 'Unemployed graduates and other priority groups, with women a priority in every group.', icon: 'users' },
      { label: 'How We Reach Them', title: 'Government Digital Labs', text: 'Blended learning in government labs you already run; lab access and seats are confirmed per program.', icon: 'landmark' },
      { label: 'What We Deliver', title: 'Trainers and Curriculum', text: 'Training of Trainers, curriculum and manuals, then preparation for industry certification.', icon: 'award' },
      { label: 'Where They Work', title: 'Placement and Monthly Reports', text: `Placement support into local, remote and global work, with monthly reports to the agency and follow-up surveys at ${TRACER_MONTHS} after each course, as agreed in the program scope.`, icon: 'globe' },
    ]}
    link={{ label: 'See How YouthWIDE Works', href: LINKS.youthwide }}
  />

  <!-- C7 · Proof: the trial and survey results are the tiles above; this strip links the full evidence (WP35, cycle-12 review) -->
  <PairedEvidence
    id="proof-band"
    eyebrow="The Evidence"
    title="See the Evidence Behind the Results"
    items={[]}
    broader={[
      { label: 'Read the Trial Results', href: LINKS.trial },
      { label: 'Impact Survey 2026', href: LINKS.outcomes },
    ]}
    broaderLabel="The evidence behind the results"
  />
  <!-- WP37 (cycle-14 review): the Her Power band (WP29) that followed the table is gone: the glance tile and the table's first row state what it showed, and the case study is a text link under the table. -->
  <ContractTable title="What Each Government Contract Covers" lead="What CodersTrust does under each contract, and the training scope it covers." more={{ label: 'Read the Her Power Case Study', href: LINKS.caseHerPower }} />

  <!-- C8 · Features to benefits. WP39 (cycle-16 review): three short cards in place of the concern / inclusion / benefit matrix, in the order of the three pains in C2 (graduates struggle to find work; budgets are measured by
       enrollment, not earnings; trainer and curriculum capacity takes time to build). The sentences keep the matrix rows' words; they rest on PD-10 and PD-01 (tracks from employer demand, mentoring and placement support), PD-11 and PD-08
       (the pilot scorecard and its cost per completer and per verified outcome, monthly reports), GV-11, PD-06 and PD-07 (Training of Trainers, curriculum and manuals, certified local facilitators, limited connectivity). -->
  <IncludesBand
    title="What Your Agency Gets"
    cards={[
      // WP62 (cycle-34 review, Gemini): "... mentoring and placement support toward local, remote and global work." (it said "... that point completers toward paid work."), the phrase the mechanism cards use for where placement leads.
      { icon: 'award', title: 'Certification Tracks and Placement Support', text: 'Certification tracks chosen from employer demand, then mentoring and placement support toward local, remote and global work.', facts: ['PD-10', 'PD-01'] },
      { icon: 'chart-bar', title: 'Spending Measured Against Outcomes', text: 'A pilot scorecard with cost per completer and per verified outcome, and monthly reports. Your budget is assessed against the agreed scorecard.', facts: ['PD-11', 'PD-08'] },
      { icon: 'landmark', title: 'Capacity Designed to Stay in Your System', text: 'Where included in your program scope: Training of Trainers, curriculum and manuals; certified local facilitators; and blended learning in existing labs that works with limited connectivity, so rural labs can take part.', facts: ['GV-11', 'PD-06', 'PD-07'] },
    ]}
  />

  <!-- C9 · Objections -->
  <FAQBand
    audience="governments"
    ids={FAQ_IDS}
    title="Questions Ministries Ask"
  />

  <!-- C10 · Differentiation: the positioning statement opens C6; the comparison table is on Home and Investors only (WP30c). -->

  <!-- C11 · Offer -->
  <OfferCards eyebrow="Options" title="Choose Where to Start" lead={PROGRAM_SIZES_NOTE_BRIEFING} cards={funderOptionCards()} recommended={0} />
  <SubBand tone="airy" pull>
    <EvidenceList
      heading="Services We Can Scope for an Agency"
      items={[
        { facts: ['GV-11'], text: 'Curriculum development, manuals and Training of Trainers.' },
        { facts: ['GV-05'], text: 'Training for staff on the learning platform and lab operations.' },
      ]}
    />
  </SubBand>

  <!-- C12 · Risk reversal (immediately before C13). WP34 (cycle-11 review): the four diligence cards repeated what this page's bands state, so one item and a link stand in -->
  <DueDiligenceView brief id="due-diligence" />
  <RiskReversalBand
    title="Phased Rollout, with Review Gates"
    points={[
      { icon: 'rocket', title: 'Pilot First', text: 'We start with a pilot, not a national rollout.' },
      { icon: 'target', title: 'Review Gates', text: 'Thresholds are agreed with the agency before the first cohort; a missed threshold triggers a corrective plan.' },
      { icon: 'handshake', title: 'The Agency Decides on Expansion', text: 'You choose whether and how far to scale.' },
      { icon: 'shield', title: 'Independent Evaluation Welcome', text: 'We support third-party evaluation of outcomes.' },
    ]}
  />

  <!-- C13 · Call to action -->
  <CTABand
    id="cta"
    heading="Let’s Plan a First Phase with Your Ministry"
    cta={{ label: primary.label, href: primary.href }}
    secondary={{ label: 'See YouthWIDE for Governments', href: LINKS.nationwide }}
  >
    <SessionAgenda lead="governments" />
  </CTABand>

  <!-- C14 · Next steps -->
  <StepsBand
    steps={[
      { title: 'Briefing', text: 'A briefing for your ministry or agency, shaped to its procurement timeline.' },
      { title: 'Co-design with the agency', text: 'Priority groups, tracks, labs and the pilot scorecard are agreed together.' },
      { title: 'Agreement', text: 'Scope and schedule follow the agency’s own procurement timeline.' },
      { title: 'Launch in Existing Labs', text: 'Seats are confirmed per program.' },
    ]}
  />

  <PageEnhancements />
</LandingLayout>
